WEBVTT

00:00:04.008 --> 00:00:08.937
Typecase writes in Markdown: plain text with a few characters for formatting.

00:00:09.237 --> 00:00:22.508
Why? Your texts stay ordinary text files. Any app on any computer can open them, today and in twenty years. And you write without fiddling with layout: the look comes later, when you export to PDF or Word.

00:00:22.809 --> 00:00:26.501
You don't have to learn the characters first. Here are the few you need.

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:30.066
A hash sign starts a heading, two of them a smaller one.

00:00:30.604 --> 00:00:37.538
Two asterisks around a word make it bold, one makes it italic. The characters stay, but fade into the background.

00:00:37.839 --> 00:00:42.619
A dash starts a list. Press Enter, and the next item begins by itself.

00:00:44.594 --> 00:00:51.593
Rather not type the characters? Select a word and use the bar at the bottom. It also turns a line into a heading.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:01:00.232
Or use the keyboard: Ctrl+B for bold, Ctrl+I for italic, Ctrl+1 to 3 for headings.

00:01:00.532 --> 00:01:03.734
That's all you need. Everything else is just writing.

00:01:04.634 --> 00:01:07.366
Typecase. Free software, made for GNOME.

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:10.248
With love, by kleinod.studio.
